{{also|EDGAR}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From the name of early English kings, {{etyl|ang}} {{term|ēad||rich|lang=ang}} + {{term|gār||spear|lang=ang}}.

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun}}

# {{given name|male|from=Old English}}.
# {{surname|patronymic|from=given names}}

====Quotations====
* {{RQ:Shakespeare Lear|II}}: Scene 1:
*: What? did my father's godson seek your life? He whom my father nam'd? your '''Edgar'''?
* '''2000''' [[w:Margaret Atwood|Margaret Atwood]], ''The Blind Assassin'', Random House, ISBN 0385720955, page 62:
*: My father was the eldest of three sons, each of whom was given Adelia's idea of a high-toned name: Norval and '''Edgar''' and Percival, Arthurian revival with a hint of Wagner. 

